_chem.ALLOW_RADICAL[0] = True  # radical SMILES in figures

PROBLEMS = [
 (59, ("(JEE Advanced, integer type) 2-Methylbutane is monochlorinated with Cl<sub>2</sub>/hν at 25 °C. Take the relative reactivities per hydrogen as primary : secondary : tertiary = 1 : 3.8 : 5.0. (a) How many constitutional isomers of C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>11</sub>Cl are formed? (b) What percentage of the product mixture is 2-chloro-3-methylbutane? (c) Treating the selectivity as a pure difference in free energy of activation, calculate ΔΔG<sup>‡</sup> (tertiary vs primary C–H abstraction) at 298 K in kJ mol<sup>−1</sup>.",
      None, "(a) 4; (b) ≈ 35 %; (c) ≈ 4.0 kJ mol<sup>−1</sup>",
      "H count: 6 primary H on the two CH<sub>3</sub> groups at C-2 (gives 1-chloro-2-methylbutane), 3 primary H on C-4 (1-chloro-3-methylbutane), 2 secondary H on C-3 (2-chloro-3-methylbutane), 1 tertiary H (2-chloro-2-methylbutane): four constitutional isomers. Weighted yields: 6 × 1 = 6; 3 × 1 = 3; 2 × 3.8 = 7.6; 1 × 5.0 = 5.0; total 21.6, so 2-chloro-3-methylbutane = 7.6/21.6 = 35.2 %. (c) ΔΔG<sup>‡</sup> = RT ln 5 = 8.314 × 298 × 1.609 = 3.99 kJ mol<sup>−1</sup>. ⚑ Equating per-H rate ratio to ΔΔG<sup>‡</sup> assumes equal pre-exponential factors; for Br• (1 : 1640 at 400 K) the same treatment gives RT ln 1640 = 24.6 kJ mol<sup>−1</sup>, which is why bromination is so much more selective.")),

 (60, ("(Thermochemistry, BDE data) Bond dissociation enthalpies (kJ mol<sup>−1</sup>): CH<sub>3</sub>–H 439; H–Cl 431; H–Br 366; Cl–Cl 243; Br–Br 193; CH<sub>3</sub>–Cl 351; CH<sub>3</sub>–Br 293. For the chain reaction CH<sub>4</sub> + X<sub>2</sub> → CH<sub>3</sub>X + HX (X = Cl, Br) calculate ΔH for each of the two propagation steps and for the overall reaction. Then estimate the ratio of rate constants k(Br•)/k(Cl•) for hydrogen abstraction from CH<sub>4</sub> at 400 K if E<sub>a</sub>(Cl•) ≈ 16 kJ mol<sup>−1</sup>, E<sub>a</sub>(Br•) ≈ ΔH of the step, and the A factors are equal.",
      None, "Cl: +8, −108, overall −100; Br: +73, −100, overall −27 kJ mol<sup>−1</sup>; ratio ≈ 4 × 10<sup>−8</sup>",
      "Step 1 (X• + CH<sub>4</sub> → HX + CH<sub>3</sub>•): ΔH = 439 − D(H–X) = +8 (Cl), +73 (Br). Step 2 (CH<sub>3</sub>• + X<sub>2</sub> → CH<sub>3</sub>X + X•): ΔH = D(X–X) − D(CH<sub>3</sub>–X) = 243 − 351 = −108 (Cl); 193 − 293 = −100 (Br). Overall: −100 and −27 kJ mol<sup>−1</sup> (check: 439 + 243 − 351 − 431 = −100; 439 + 193 − 293 − 366 = −27). For Br• the barrier cannot be lower than the endothermicity (+73), so k(Br)/k(Cl) = exp[−(73 − 16)×10<sup>3</sup>/(8.314 × 400)] = exp(−17.1) ≈ 3.6 × 10<sup>−8</sup>. ⚑ E<sub>a</sub>(Br•) = ΔH is the lower limit used as an estimate (Evans–Polanyi style).")),

 (61, ("(Olympiad style, stereochemistry) (S)-1-Chloro-2-methylbutane is further chlorinated with Cl<sub>2</sub>/hν and every possible dichloride (substitution of one C–H) is formed. Of the constitutional isomers of C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>10</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub> obtained, how many are formed as optically active (non-racemic) material? Assume no reaction at the existing C–Cl bond.",
      S([("(S)-1-chloro-2-methylbutane", "C[C@@H](CC)CCl")]),
      "3 (1,1-, 1,3- and 1,4-dichloro-2-methylbutane)",
      "Five constitutional dichlorides arise by abstraction at C-1, C-2, C-3, C-4 or the C-2 methyl. Abstraction at C-2 removes the stereocentre hydrogen, giving a planar radical and racemic 1,2-dichloro-2-methylbutane (inactive). Chlorination at the C-2 methyl gives ClCH<sub>2</sub>CH(CH<sub>2</sub>Cl)CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>, in which C-2 carries two identical CH<sub>2</sub>Cl groups: achiral. Chlorination at C-1 (1,1-dichloro), C-3 (1,3-dichloro, two diastereomers, which differ in the new centre and are unequal) and C-4 (1,4-dichloro) leaves the C-2 centre untouched, so all three are optically active. Answer 3. ⚑ The CIP descriptor of retained centres can change with substituents; the spatial arrangement is retained.")),

 (62, ("(JEE Advanced, integer type) 1-Octene is treated with one equivalent of NBS and a trace of peroxide in refluxing CCl<sub>4</sub>. Counting each enantiomer and each geometrical isomer separately, how many distinct monobromo compounds can be formed through the allylic radical?",
      S([("1-octene", "C=CCCCCCC"), ("3-bromooct-1-ene", "C=CC(Br)CCCCC"), ("1-bromooct-2-ene (E)", "CCCCC/C=C/CBr")]),
      "4",
      "NBS supplies a low steady concentration of Br<sub>2</sub> (from HBr + NBS), so Br• abstracts the only allylic hydrogen set (C-3) and addition of Br<sub>2</sub> across C=C is suppressed. The resulting allyl radical CH<sub>2</sub>=CH–CH•–C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>11</sub> ↔ •CH<sub>2</sub>–CH=CH–C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>11</sub> is brominated at either terminus: at C-3 gives 3-bromooct-1-ene, a stereocentre formed as a racemate (2 compounds); at C-1 gives 1-bromooct-2-ene as E and Z (2 compounds). Total 4. ⚑ The internal (E)-1-bromo isomer usually predominates because the more substituted alkene is more stable and the allylic bromides can interconvert under the conditions.")),

 (63, ("(JEE Advanced, multiple correct) Choose the correct statement(s). (A) The tertiary C–H bond of isobutane (≈ 96 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>) is weaker than the secondary C–H of propane (≈ 99 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>). (B) The phenyl radical is a π-radical delocalised over the ring and is therefore as stable as the benzyl radical. (C) In the allyl radical the unpaired spin density is carried by the two terminal carbons (0.5 each in simple Hückel theory) and is zero on C-2. (D) The benzylic C–H bond of ethylbenzene (≈ 85 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>) is weaker than that of toluene (≈ 90 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>). (E) The cyclopropyl radical is more stable than the isopropyl radical because angle strain is relieved on forming it. (F) The methyl radical is essentially planar, whereas the tert-butyl radical is slightly pyramidal.",
      None, "A, C, D, F",
      "(A) True: weaker bond means more stable radical (hyperconjugation and polarisability). (B) False: the phenyl radical is a σ radical, with the electron in an sp<sup>2</sup> orbital in the ring plane, orthogonal to the π system; its C–H BDE is ≈ 112 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>. (C) True: the non-bonding allyl MO has a node at C-2. (D) True: the extra α-methyl gives additional hyperconjugative stabilisation, so NBS attacks ethyl groups faster than methyl groups on arenes. (E) False: cyclopropyl C–H BDE ≈ 106 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>, the radical is σ-like and the ring resists planarity. (F) True; ⚑ the pyramidalisation of t-Bu• is shallow (small inversion barrier), and some texts call it nearly planar.")),

 (64, ("(Mechanism and stereochemistry) 1-Methylcyclohexene reacts with HBr in the presence of benzoyl peroxide. Give the constitution of the product, the number of stereoisomers possible for it, and the stereoisomer predominantly formed. Why do HCl and HI not give anti-Markovnikov addition under the same conditions?",
      S([("1-methylcyclohexene", "CC1=CCCCC1"), ("product", "CC1CCCCC1Br")]),
      "1-bromo-2-methylcyclohexane; 4 stereoisomers; mainly trans (anti addition)",
      "Br• adds to the less substituted alkene carbon (C-2) so that the more stable tertiary radical forms at C-1; the radical then abstracts H from HBr (chain propagation, ΔH ≈ −44 kJ mol<sup>−1</sup>) giving 1-bromo-2-methylcyclohexane with two stereocentres: 4 stereoisomers (cis pair and trans pair of enantiomers). Hydrogen delivery to the tertiary radical occurs anti to the bromine (axial attack on the favoured conformer), so the racemic trans isomer predominates (Goering). ⚑ Under thermodynamic conditions (reversible Br• addition) the cis/trans ratio can change. With HCl the H-abstraction step is endothermic (H–Cl 431 vs C–H ≈ 410 kJ mol<sup>−1</sup>); with HI the I• addition to the alkene is endothermic and reversible, and I• atoms simply combine to I<sub>2</sub>.")),

 (65, ("(Quantitative, HX addition) Use these average data (kJ mol<sup>−1</sup>): C=C π bond 265; D(C–Cl) 339; D(C–Br) 285; D(C–I) 213; D(H–Cl) 431; D(H–Br) 366; D(H–I) 298; D(secondary C–H) 410. For the chain addition of HX to propene (X• adds to CH<sub>2</sub>, then the secondary radical abstracts H from HX) compute ΔH of the addition step and of the hydrogen-transfer step for X = Cl, Br, I, and the overall ΔH. Which HX can sustain a radical chain and what is the overall ΔH for it?",
      None, "only HBr; ΔH(overall) = −64 kJ mol<sup>−1</sup>",
      "Addition step ΔH = 265 − D(C–X): Cl −74, Br −20, I +52. Hydrogen-transfer step ΔH = D(H–X) − 410: Cl +21, Br −44, I −112. Overall: Cl −53, Br −64, I −60 kJ mol<sup>−1</sup> (all favourable overall, but this is not sufficient). For HCl the second step is endothermic, so hydrogen transfer is slow compared with competing steps (telomerisation, termination); for HI the first step is endothermic, so I• addition is reversible and the chain is not sustained. Only HBr has both steps exothermic (−20 and −44), giving an efficient chain. ⚑ Average bond energies are approximate; the conclusion rests on signs and magnitudes, not on the exact numbers.")),

 (66, ("(Kharasch addition, Olympiad style) 1-Octene is heated with CCl<sub>4</sub> and a catalytic amount of dibenzoyl peroxide. (a) Give the 1:1 adduct and its name. (b) The intermediate carbon radical can either abstract Cl from CCl<sub>4</sub> (rate constant k<sub>tr</sub>) or add to another octene (k<sub>p</sub>). With k<sub>tr</sub>/k<sub>p</sub> = 2.0, [CCl<sub>4</sub>] = 4.0 M and [octene] = 0.50 M (both constant), what percentage of the product chains are the 1:1 adduct and what percentage are the 2:1 telomer?",
      S([("1:1 adduct", "ClC(Cl)(Cl)CC(Cl)CCCCCC")]),
      "1,1,1,3-tetrachlorononane; ≈ 94 % 1:1 and ≈ 5.5 % 2:1",
      "Initiation gives •CCl<sub>3</sub> (peroxide-derived radicals abstract Cl from CCl<sub>4</sub>). •CCl<sub>3</sub> adds to the terminal CH<sub>2</sub> of the alkene giving the secondary radical, which abstracts Cl from CCl<sub>4</sub> and regenerates •CCl<sub>3</sub>: Cl<sub>3</sub>C–CH<sub>2</sub>–CHCl–C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>13</sub>, 1,1,1,3-tetrachlorononane (9 carbons). The probability of transfer at each stage is p = k<sub>tr</sub>[CCl<sub>4</sub>]/(k<sub>tr</sub>[CCl<sub>4</sub>] + k<sub>p</sub>[M]) = 8.0/8.5 = 0.941. The 1:1 adduct fraction is p = 94.1 %; the 2:1 telomer is (1 − p)p = 0.0588 × 0.941 = 5.5 %. ⚑ Treated as geometric (Mayo-type) distribution with constant concentrations.")),

 (67, ("(Polymerisation kinetics) Styrene (4.0 M) is polymerised in solution at 60 °C using AIBN (0.010 M, assumed constant). Data: k<sub>d</sub> = 1.0 × 10<sup>−5</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>, f = 0.60, k<sub>p</sub> = 3.4 × 10<sup>2</sup> L mol<sup>−1</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>, k<sub>t</sub> = 6.0 × 10<sup>7</sup> L mol<sup>−1</sup> s<sup>−1</sup> (defined by R<sub>t</sub> = 2k<sub>t</sub>[P•]<sup>2</sup>). Termination is by combination only. Calculate (a) the steady-state radical concentration, (b) R<sub>p</sub>, (c) the kinetic chain length ν and the number-average degree of polymerisation, (d) M<sub>n</sub> (styrene = 104.15 g mol<sup>−1</sup>), and (e) the time to 10 % conversion.",
      None, "[P•] = 3.2 × 10<sup>−8</sup> M; R<sub>p</sub> = 4.3 × 10<sup>−5</sup> M s<sup>−1</sup>; ν ≈ 358; X<sub>n</sub> ≈ 717; M<sub>n</sub> ≈ 7.5 × 10<sup>4</sup>; t ≈ 2.7 h",
      "R<sub>i</sub> = 2fk<sub>d</sub>[I] = 2 × 0.60 × 1.0×10<sup>−5</sup> × 0.010 = 1.2 × 10<sup>−7</sup> M s<sup>−1</sup>. Steady state R<sub>i</sub> = R<sub>t</sub>: [P•] = (R<sub>i</sub>/2k<sub>t</sub>)<sup>1/2</sup> = (1.0×10<sup>−15</sup>)<sup>1/2</sup> = 3.16 × 10<sup>−8</sup> M. R<sub>p</sub> = k<sub>p</sub>[M][P•] = 340 × 4.0 × 3.16×10<sup>−8</sup> = 4.30 × 10<sup>−5</sup> M s<sup>−1</sup>. ν = R<sub>p</sub>/R<sub>i</sub> = 358; combination doubles it: X<sub>n</sub> = 2ν = 717, M<sub>n</sub> = 717 × 104.15 = 7.47 × 10<sup>4</sup>. Apparent first-order rate constant = k<sub>p</sub>[P•] = 1.07 × 10<sup>−5</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>; t = ln(1/0.9)/1.07×10<sup>−5</sup> = 9.8 × 10<sup>3</sup> s = 2.7 h. ⚑ Conventions for k<sub>t</sub> differ (R<sub>t</sub> = k<sub>t</sub>[P•]<sup>2</sup> in some texts); doubling [I] raises R<sub>p</sub> by √2 and lowers ν by √2.")),

 (68, ("(Chain transfer and inhibition) In the bulk polymerisation of styrene at 60 °C, [M] = 4.0 M. Without transfer agent 1/X<sub>n</sub> = 2.0 × 10<sup>−4</sup>. Dodecanethiol (RSH) has chain-transfer constant C<sub>S</sub> = 15. (a) Calculate X<sub>n</sub> when [RSH] = 2.0 × 10<sup>−3</sup> M. (b) What [RSH] is needed for X<sub>n</sub> = 50? (c) How does the thiol change R<sub>p</sub> and why? (d) In another run with R<sub>i</sub> = 1.2 × 10<sup>−7</sup> M s<sup>−1</sup>, benzoquinone (5.0 × 10<sup>−5</sup> M) is added; assuming each inhibitor molecule traps two radicals, find the induction period.",
      None, "(a) ≈ 130; (b) ≈ 5.3 × 10<sup>−3</sup> M; (c) R<sub>p</sub> unchanged; (d) ≈ 833 s (14 min)",
      "Mayo equation: 1/X<sub>n</sub> = 1/X<sub>n0</sub> + C<sub>S</sub>[S]/[M]. (a) 1/X<sub>n</sub> = 2.0×10<sup>−4</sup> + 15 × 2.0×10<sup>−3</sup>/4.0 = 7.7 × 10<sup>−3</sup>, X<sub>n</sub> = 130. (b) 1/50 = 0.0200; 0.0198 = 15[S]/4.0, so [S] = 5.28 × 10<sup>−3</sup> M. (c) The thiyl radical RS• re-initiates styrene polymerisation quickly, so the number of growing chains is unchanged and R<sub>p</sub> is unaffected: this is pure chain transfer, not retardation. (d) Radicals are scavenged until the inhibitor is consumed: τ = n[Z]/R<sub>i</sub> = 2 × 5.0×10<sup>−5</sup>/1.2×10<sup>−7</sup> = 833 s. ⚑ The stoichiometric factor n for quinones is not always exactly 2 and depends on the radical.")),

 (69, ("(Copolymerisation) Styrene (M<sub>1</sub>) and methyl methacrylate (M<sub>2</sub>) copolymerise radically with r<sub>1</sub> = 0.52 and r<sub>2</sub> = 0.46 (taken as given). (a) Find the mole fraction of styrene in the initial copolymer from an equimolar feed. (b) Find the azeotropic feed composition f<sub>1</sub>. (c) What is the mean sequence length of styrene units in the equimolar feed, and what does r<sub>1</sub>r<sub>2</sub> tell about the structure?",
      None, "(a) F<sub>1</sub> ≈ 0.51; (b) f<sub>1</sub> ≈ 0.53; (c) 1.52; r<sub>1</sub>r<sub>2</sub> = 0.24, tendency towards alternation",
      "Mayo–Lewis: F<sub>1</sub> = (r<sub>1</sub>f<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> + f<sub>1</sub>f<sub>2</sub>)/(r<sub>1</sub>f<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> + 2f<sub>1</sub>f<sub>2</sub> + r<sub>2</sub>f<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>) = (0.13 + 0.25)/(0.13 + 0.50 + 0.115) = 0.38/0.745 = 0.510. Azeotrope: f<sub>1</sub> = (1 − r<sub>2</sub>)/(2 − r<sub>1</sub> − r<sub>2</sub>) = 0.54/1.02 = 0.529. Mean sequence length of M<sub>1</sub> = 1 + r<sub>1</sub>[M<sub>1</sub>]/[M<sub>2</sub>] = 1.52. Since both r &lt; 1 and r<sub>1</sub>r<sub>2</sub> = 0.239 &lt; 1, each radical prefers adding the other monomer; the copolymer is rather random with alternating tendency, and composition barely drifts near the azeotrope. ⚑ Reactivity ratios vary with temperature and medium.")),

 (70, ("(Cyclisation regiochemistry, JEE Advanced integer type) For which of the following radicals is the major cyclic product (after H-atom transfer from Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH at low tin hydride concentration) a six-membered ring? (a) hex-5-en-1-yl; (b) 5-methylhex-5-en-1-yl; (c) hept-6-en-1-yl; (d) hept-6-en-2-yl. Give the number of such radicals, and for (d) state the main stereochemical outcome.",
      S([("(a)", "C=CCCC[CH2]"), ("(b)", "C=C(C)CCC[CH2]"), ("(c)", "C=CCCCC[CH2]"), ("(d)", "C=CCCC[CH]C")]),
      "2 ((b) and (c)); (d) gives mainly cis-1,2-dimethylcyclopentane",
      "(a) 5-exo (k ≈ 2 × 10<sup>5</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>) wins over 6-endo because of better SOMO–π* overlap in the chair-like Beckwith TS: methylcyclopentane. (b) A methyl on C-5 hinders 5-exo attack at the substituted carbon, so 6-endo, giving the tertiary 1-methylcyclohexyl radical, predominates (≈ 2 : 1). (c) For the hept-6-enyl radical 6-exo is still favoured over 7-endo, giving the cyclohexylmethyl radical (about 50 times slower than 5-exo). (d) 5-Exo cyclisation; a 1-substituent in the chair-like TS prefers cis stereochemistry, so cis-1,2-dimethylcyclopentane is the major product (Beckwith rule: 1- and 3-substituted give cis, 2- and 4- give trans). Answer: 2. ⚑ Ratios are approximate and temperature-dependent; the cis preference is modest (about 2 : 1).")),

 (71, ("(Neophyl rearrangement as a slow radical clock) Neophyl bromide, PhC(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Br, is reduced by Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH (AIBN, 25 °C). The neophyl radical undergoes 1,2-aryl migration with k<sub>r</sub> = 7.6 × 10<sup>2</sup> s<sup>−1</sup> (taken as given), while trapping by Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH has k<sub>H</sub> = 2.4 × 10<sup>6</sup> M<sup>−1</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>. (a) Name the two hydrocarbons formed and the intermediate involved in the migration. (b) What percentage of rearranged hydrocarbon is formed at [Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH] = 0.10 M (assume constant)? (c) At what [Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH] are the two hydrocarbons formed in equal amounts?",
      S([("neophyl radical", "[CH2]C(C)(C)c1ccccc1"), ("rearranged radical", "C[C](C)Cc1ccccc1")]),
      "tert-butylbenzene (unrearranged) and isobutylbenzene (rearranged); (b) ≈ 0.32 %; (c) ≈ 3.2 × 10<sup>−4</sup> M",
      "The primary neophyl radical attacks its own ipso carbon (3-exo) to give a spiro cyclopropyl-cyclohexadienyl radical; ring opening on the other side gives the tertiary radical (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>C•–CH<sub>2</sub>Ph, driven by primary to tertiary radical conversion. Direct reduction of the neophyl radical gives tert-butylbenzene; reduction of the rearranged radical gives isobutylbenzene (2-methylpropylbenzene). Competition: rearranged/unrearranged = k<sub>r</sub>/(k<sub>H</sub>[SnH]) = 760/(2.4×10<sup>5</sup>) = 3.2 × 10<sup>−3</sup>, i.e. 0.316 % of the total. Equal amounts when k<sub>H</sub>[SnH] = k<sub>r</sub>: [SnH] = 760/2.4×10<sup>6</sup> = 3.2 × 10<sup>−4</sup> M. Because k<sub>r</sub> is small, the neophyl clock needs very low tin hydride (or syringe-pump addition) to be useful. ⚑ The ratio assumes the rearranged radical is not appreciably reverted.")),

 (72, ("(Barton–McCombie and a built-in clock) The S-methyl xanthate of hept-6-en-2-ol is heated with Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH (slow addition, so that [Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH] stays constant) and AIBN in benzene at 80 °C. Take k<sub>c</sub> (5-exo cyclisation of the secondary radical) = 2.0 × 10<sup>5</sup> s<sup>−1</sup> and k<sub>H</sub> = 2.0 × 10<sup>6</sup> M<sup>−1</sup> s<sup>−1</sup> (assumed values). (a) Write the two products and the tin by-product. (b) Calculate the percentage of cyclised product at [Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH] = 0.010 M and at 1.0 M. (c) Which tin-containing species adds to the C=S bond, and what drives the fragmentation?",
      S([("xanthate", "C=CCCCC(C)OC(=S)SC"), ("uncyclised", "C=CCCCCC"), ("cyclised", "CC1CCCC1C")]),
      "hept-1-ene and 1,2-dimethylcyclopentane (cis major); 91 % cyclised at 0.010 M, 9.1 % at 1.0 M; Bu<sub>3</sub>Sn–S–C(=O)SMe by-product",
      "AIBN generates Bu<sub>3</sub>Sn•, which attacks sulfur of C=S to give the stabilised adduct radical; β-scission cleaves the C–O bond, forming the secondary radical CH<sub>3</sub>CH•(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub> and Bu<sub>3</sub>SnSC(=O)SMe (strong C=O and Sn–S bonds drive it). The radical either takes H from Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH (giving hept-1-ene and regenerating Bu<sub>3</sub>Sn•) or cyclises 5-exo then is reduced to 1,2-dimethylcyclopentane. Ratio cyclised/uncyclised = k<sub>c</sub>/(k<sub>H</sub>[SnH]): at 0.010 M, 2.0×10<sup>5</sup>/2.0×10<sup>4</sup> = 10, so 10/11 = 91 % cyclised; at 1.0 M, 2.0×10<sup>5</sup>/2.0×10<sup>6</sup> = 0.10, so 0.1/1.1 = 9.1 %. One equivalent of Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH is consumed per xanthate. ⚑ cis-1,2-dimethylcyclopentane is the major diastereomer (Beckwith); the rate constants are given values for the exercise.")),

 (73, ("(Giese reaction) tert-Butyl iodide and methyl acrylate (1.0 M) are treated with Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH (kept at 0.020 M by slow addition) and AIBN. (a) Give the main product. (b) Take k<sub>add</sub>(t-Bu• + acrylate) = 1.0 × 10<sup>6</sup> M<sup>−1</sup> s<sup>−1</sup> and k<sub>H</sub>(t-Bu• + Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH) = 2.0 × 10<sup>6</sup> M<sup>−1</sup> s<sup>−1</sup> (assumed); calculate the fraction of t-Bu• that gives the adduct. (c) Why does t-Bu• add to acrylate but not appreciably to 1-hexene, while •CH<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>Et adds readily to 1-hexene?",
      S([("product", "COC(=O)CCC(C)(C)C")]),
      "methyl 4,4-dimethylpentanoate; ≈ 96 %; polarity matching (SOMO–LUMO / SOMO–HOMO)",
      "Bu<sub>3</sub>Sn• abstracts I from t-BuI, t-Bu• adds to the β-carbon of acrylate, giving the α-ester radical, which takes H from Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH to give t-Bu–CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>Me (methyl 4,4-dimethylpentanoate). Partition: addition 1.0×10<sup>6</sup> × 1.0 = 1.0×10<sup>6</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>; premature reduction 2.0×10<sup>6</sup> × 0.020 = 4.0×10<sup>4</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>; adduct fraction = 1.0×10<sup>6</sup>/1.04×10<sup>6</sup> = 96 %. The nucleophilic (high-SOMO) tertiary radical reacts quickly only with electron-poor alkenes (low LUMO); the electrophilic radical from the ester has a low SOMO and adds to electron-rich alkenes. High [alkene], low [SnH] favours the Giese adduct. ⚑ Real rate constants vary with temperature and solvent.")),

 (74, ("(Autoxidation and the Hock process) Cumene (C<sub>9</sub>H<sub>12</sub>, 120.19 g mol<sup>−1</sup>) is oxidised by O<sub>2</sub> to the hydroperoxide, then cleaved with acid to phenol and acetone. (a) Write the two propagation steps. (b) With D(C–H) of cumene = 84 and D(ROO–H) = 88 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>, find ΔH of the hydrogen transfer step. (c) What mass of phenol and of acetone (94.11 and 58.08 g mol<sup>−1</sup>) forms from 1.00 kg cumene at an overall yield of 85 %? (d) BHT (n = 2 radicals per molecule, 1.0 × 10<sup>−3</sup> M) is present while R<sub>i</sub> = 2.0 × 10<sup>−8</sup> M s<sup>−1</sup>; estimate the induction period.",
      S([("cumene", "CC(C)c1ccccc1"), ("cumene hydroperoxide", "CC(C)(OO)c1ccccc1")]),
      "(b) −4 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>; (c) ≈ 666 g phenol, ≈ 411 g acetone; (d) ≈ 1.0 × 10<sup>5</sup> s (≈ 28 h)",
      "(a) R• + O<sub>2</sub> → ROO• (diffusion controlled); ROO• + RH → ROOH + R•, where R• = PhC•(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>. (b) ΔH = D(C–H) − D(O–H) = 84 − 88 = −4 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>, so the weak tertiary benzylic C–H bond makes the chain feasible. Acid then protonates the OOH; phenyl migrates to oxygen (Hock rearrangement) with loss of water to give a stabilised oxocarbenium ion, hydration gives a hemiketal, which collapses to PhOH + (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>C=O. (c) 1000/120.19 = 8.320 mol; × 0.85 = 7.072 mol; phenol 7.072 × 94.11 = 666 g; acetone 7.072 × 58.08 = 411 g. (d) τ = n[AH]/R<sub>i</sub> = 2 × 1.0×10<sup>−3</sup>/2.0×10<sup>−8</sup> = 1.0 × 10<sup>5</sup> s = 27.8 h. ⚑ BDE values are rounded literature numbers; the BHT stoichiometry n = 2 is idealised.")),

 (75, ("(JEE Advanced, match the column) Match each decarboxylative method applied to pentanoic acid (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>H or its derivative) with the main organic product. Column I: (P) silver salt, Br<sub>2</sub>/CCl<sub>4</sub>, heat; (Q) sodium salt, electrolysis in methanol; (R) N-hydroxy-2-thiopyridone (Barton) ester, hν, t-BuSH; (S) the same Barton ester, hν, CCl<sub>4</sub>; (T) Pb(OAc)<sub>4</sub>, Cu(OAc)<sub>2</sub>, heat. Column II: (1) butane; (2) 1-bromobutane; (3) octane; (4) 1-chlorobutane; (5) but-1-ene.",
      S([("pentanoic acid", "CCCCC(=O)O")]),
      "P–2, Q–3, R–1, S–4, T–5",
      "(P) Hunsdiecker: acyl hypobromite, RCO<sub>2</sub>• loses CO<sub>2</sub>, the butyl radical abstracts Br: 1-bromobutane. (Q) Kolbe: anodic oxidation to RCO<sub>2</sub>•, loss of CO<sub>2</sub>, radical dimerisation: octane. (R) Photolysis of the PTOC ester cleaves N–O, CO<sub>2</sub> is lost, and the butyl radical takes H from t-BuSH: butane. (S) In CCl<sub>4</sub> the butyl radical abstracts Cl: 1-chlorobutane. (T) Kochi oxidative decarboxylation: the radical is oxidised by Cu(II) to a Cu(III) alkyl and loses a β-hydrogen, giving the terminal alkene but-1-ene. ⚑ Kochi conditions can also give acetates or alkyl halides depending on the copper ligands; the alkene is the textbook outcome.")),

 (76, ("(SRN1, very hard) Sodium 2-nitropropanate reacts with 4-nitrobenzyl chloride in DMF to give 1-(2-methyl-2-nitropropyl)-4-nitrobenzene in high yield. The reaction is accelerated by light, and is strongly inhibited by 10 mol % of 1,4-dinitrobenzene or by O<sub>2</sub>. (a) How many propagation steps does the SRN1 cycle contain, and what are they? (b) After absorbing 1.25 × 10<sup>−5</sup> mol of photons (one initiating electron transfer per photon), 0.50 mmol of product has formed. Calculate the quantum yield. (c) Why does 1,4-dinitrobenzene inhibit?",
      S([("substrate", "[O-][N+](=O)c1ccc(CCl)cc1"), ("nucleophile", "CC(C)=[N+]([O-])[O-]"), ("product", "CC(C)([N+](=O)[O-])Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1")]),
      "3 propagation steps; Φ = 40; dinitrobenzene intercepts the chain-carrying radical anion",
      "Initiation: electron transfer (photo- or thermal) from the nitronate to ArCH<sub>2</sub>Cl gives the radical anion. Propagation: (1) ArCH<sub>2</sub>Cl•− → ArCH<sub>2</sub>• + Cl−; (2) ArCH<sub>2</sub>• + (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>C=NO<sub>2</sub>− → [ArCH<sub>2</sub>C(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NO<sub>2</sub>]•− (C-alkylation); (3) this radical anion transfers an electron to a fresh molecule of ArCH<sub>2</sub>Cl, giving product and regenerating the substrate radical anion. Quantum yield Φ = 0.50 × 10<sup>−3</sup>/1.25 × 10<sup>−5</sup> = 40, i.e. each photon launches a chain of average length 40 before termination. 1,4-Dinitrobenzene is a better electron acceptor than the substrate: it takes the electron from the chain-carrying radical anion, producing a relatively unreactive radical anion that does not fragment, so step (3) is intercepted. O<sub>2</sub> acts likewise by trapping radicals and radical anions. ⚑ Some texts count the initiation electron transfer as step 1; the cycle itself is three steps.")),

 (77, ("(Sandmeyer and Gomberg–Bachmann) 2.00 g of 4-bromoaniline (172.0 g mol<sup>−1</sup>) is diazotised at 0 °C. Half of the resulting diazonium salt solution is treated with CuCl, the other half with benzene and aqueous NaOH. (a) Name the main product of each reaction and state the key radical steps. (b) If the Gomberg–Bachmann step gives 30 % yield (based on half of the diazonium salt), what mass of biaryl is obtained? (c) Which position of nitrobenzene is not preferentially attacked in the Gomberg–Bachmann arylation by Ar•?",
      S([("4-bromoaniline", "Nc1ccc(Br)cc1"), ("Sandmeyer product", "Clc1ccc(Br)cc1"), ("GB product", "Brc1ccc(cc1)-c1ccccc1")]),
      "1-bromo-4-chlorobenzene; 4-bromobiphenyl, 0.41 g; meta",
      "Sandmeyer: Cu(I) transfers an electron to ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>, giving Ar• + N<sub>2</sub> + Cu(II)Cl; Ar• abstracts Cl from CuCl<sub>2</sub> (ligand transfer), regenerating Cu(I): 1-bromo-4-chlorobenzene. Gomberg–Bachmann: under basic conditions the diazonium forms the diazotate Ar–N=N–O−, which gives Ar• (N<sub>2</sub> loss) that adds to benzene, giving a cyclohexadienyl radical, deprotonated/oxidised to 4-bromobiphenyl. (b) Total amine = 2.00/172.0 = 0.01163 mol; half = 0.005814 mol; 30 % gives 0.001744 mol × 233.1 g mol<sup>−1</sup> = 0.407 g. (c) Aryl radicals attack nitrobenzene ortho and para (≈ 60 : 30) in preference to meta (≈ 10 %). ⚑ Isomer ratios depend on conditions. (Using the whole 2.00 g in this step would give 0.81 g.)")),

 (78, ("(EPR) Predict the number of resolved hyperfine lines for each of the following radicals in solution, assuming fast methyl rotation and that every inequivalent set of protons gives a resolved splitting: (i) CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>• (a<sub>β</sub>(3H) = 2.69 mT, a<sub>α</sub>(2H) = 2.24 mT); (ii) (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH• (a(6H) = 2.47 mT, a(1H) = 2.21 mT); (iii) PhCH<sub>2</sub>• (a(CH<sub>2</sub>) = 1.63, a<sub>o</sub>(2H) = 0.51, a<sub>m</sub>(2H) = 0.18, a<sub>p</sub>(1H) = 0.62 mT); (iv) CD<sub>3</sub>• (a(H) in CH<sub>3</sub>• = 2.30 mT; γ<sub>D</sub>/γ<sub>H</sub> = 0.1535, I(D) = 1). Give also the total number of lines for the four radicals and the hyperfine coupling for CD<sub>3</sub>•.",
      S([("(i)", "C[CH2]"), ("(ii)", "C[CH]C"), ("(iii)", "[CH2]c1ccccc1"), ("(iv)", "[2H][C]([2H])[2H]")]),
      "12, 14, 54 and 7 lines (total 87); a(D) ≈ 0.35 mT",
      "Lines = ∏(2nI + 1) over sets. (i) 4 (quartet from 3 H) × 3 (triplet from 2 H) = 12. (ii) 7 (septet from 6 equivalent H) × 2 = 14. (iii) CH<sub>2</sub> gives 3, ortho 3, meta 3, para 2: 3 × 3 × 3 × 2 = 54. (iv) three I = 1 nuclei give 2(3)(1) + 1 = 7 lines in the ratio 1 : 3 : 6 : 7 : 6 : 3 : 1 (sum 27 = 3<sup>3</sup>); a(D) = 2.30 × 0.1535 = 0.353 mT. Total 12 + 14 + 54 + 7 = 87. ⚑ In practice the unresolved weak couplings (meta in benzyl) and overlap of lines may reduce the lines actually seen; the question asks for the idealised count.")),

 (79, ("(Persistent radicals) Gomberg's trityl radical exists in equilibrium with a dimer: dimer ⇌ 2 Ph<sub>3</sub>C•. In benzene at 25 °C a solution containing 0.100 mol L<sup>−1</sup> total trityl units is 2.0 % dissociated (fraction of units present as radicals). (a) Calculate K = [Ph<sub>3</sub>C•]<sup>2</sup>/[dimer]. (b) Calculate the percentage of trityl units present as radicals at a total concentration of 1.00 × 10<sup>−3</sup> mol L<sup>−1</sup>. (c) Explain, for TEMPO and for the BHT-derived phenoxyl radical, why they are persistent and name one difference between them.",
      S([("trityl", "[C](c1ccccc1)(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1"), ("TEMPO", "CC1(C)CCCC(C)(C)N1[O]"), ("BHT phenoxyl", "Cc1cc(C(C)(C)C)c([O])c(C(C)(C)C)c1")]),
      "(a) K ≈ 8.2 × 10<sup>−5</sup> M; (b) ≈ 18 %",
      "(a) With c = 0.100 M of units and 2.0 % radical: [R•] = 2.0×10<sup>−3</sup> M and [dimer] = (0.100 − 0.0020)/2 = 0.049 M, so K = (2.0×10<sup>−3</sup>)<sup>2</sup>/0.049 = 8.2 × 10<sup>−5</sup> M. (b) With x = [R•]: x<sup>2</sup>/[(c − x)/2] = K gives 2x<sup>2</sup> + Kx − Kc = 0; for c = 1.00×10<sup>−3</sup> M, x = [−K + (K<sup>2</sup> + 8Kc)<sup>1/2</sup>]/4 = 1.83×10<sup>−4</sup> M, i.e. 18 % radical (dilution favours dissociation). The dimer is not hexaphenylethane but the head-to-tail quinoid dimer, because of steric strain. (c) TEMPO: no hydrogens on the carbons α to N, so it cannot disproportionate to a nitrone and hydroxylamine, and the N–O three-electron π bond plus four methyl groups shield the centre; it is isolable. BHT phenoxyl: spin delocalisation into the ring and the two ortho tert-butyl groups shield the O, so dimerisation is slow; but, unlike TEMPO, it has hydrogens (para CH<sub>3</sub>) and eventually disproportionates (to the quinone methide), so it is persistent only for a limited time. ⚑ 'Persistent' is kinetic and conditional; K values vary with solvent and temperature.")),

 (80, ("(Hofmann–Löffler–Freytag, 1,5-HAT) N-Chloro-N-methyl-4-methylpentan-1-amine is irradiated in conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, then made basic. (a) Give the final product and the intermediate chloroamine. (b) How many atoms are in the cyclic hydrogen-transfer transition state, and why is that position (not a different C–H) attacked even though other C–H bonds are also weak? (c) What role does H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> play?",
      S([("substrate", "CN(Cl)CCCC(C)C"), ("product", "CN1CCCC1(C)C")]),
      "1,2,2-trimethylpyrrolidine; the intermediate is 4-chloro-N,4-dimethylpentan-1-amine; six-membered TS",
      "Photolysis of the protonated N–Cl bond gives the aminium radical cation CH<sub>3</sub>N•+H–R, which is electrophilic and abstracts a hydridic C–H through a six-membered, chair-like transition state (N, C-1, C-2, C-3, C-4 and H): 1,5-HAT from the δ-carbon C-4, which here is tertiary. The C-4 radical abstracts Cl from another N-chloroammonium, giving the δ-chloroamine and propagating the chain. On treatment with base, the free secondary amine displaces the tertiary chloride intramolecularly to form the five-membered ring, 1,2,2-trimethylpyrrolidine, C<sub>7</sub>H<sub>15</sub>N. Acid protonates the amine so the N-radical is an electrophilic aminium ion, and prevents hydrolysis and N-centred side reactions. Six-membered TS: 1,4-HAT (5-membered TS) is strained, and 1,6-HAT requires a less favourable seven-membered TS. ⚑ Some treat the ring closure of the tertiary chloride as SN1-like rather than SN2.")),
]
_chem.ALLOW_RADICAL[0] = _old
